  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates how land-use changes in a river valley can increase earthquake damage through liquefaction. It compares a natural pre-development landscape with a later urbanized version and then shows the consequences during shaking.\n\nInitially, the area contains a meandering river bordered by swamps and marshes over bedrock. In the developed stage, those wetlands are removed, the river is channelized, and parts of the former river bed are filled with artificial material to create buildable land. Houses and taller buildings are then constructed across this modified floodplain, including on former marshes and filled channels.\n\nThe final scene shows earthquake-induced liquefaction concentrated in these altered, softer ground areas. Buildings on reclaimed or formerly wet land tilt, sink, or collapse, while structures on more stable ground appear less affected. The labels emphasize that liquefaction is more likely in the filled channel and that buildings placed on former marshes and swamps are especially vulnerable.\n\nOverall, the image conveys a clear cause-and-effect sequence: natural wetlands and old river deposits are relatively hazardous foundation materials, and urban development that removes wetlands, straightens rivers, and adds fill can amplify earthquake risk.",
